## Answer

**Yes, $f$ is analytic.**

## Proof

We assume $P$ is a nonzero polynomial of degree $n \geq 1$ (so $u_n \neq 0$ as a power series). The key tool is the **convergent Newton–Puiseux theorem**.

> **Theorem (Convergent Newton–Puiseux).** Let $Q(T)=\sum_{i=0}^{d} a_i(z)\,T^i \in \mathbb{C}\{z\}[T]$ with $a_d\neq 0$. Then there exists a positive integer $m$ such that $Q$ splits completely over $\mathbb{C}\{z^{1/m}\}$:
> $$Q(T) = a_d(z)\prod_{j=1}^{d}\bigl(T-\varphi_j(z^{1/m})\bigr),$$
> where each $\varphi_j\in\mathbb{C}\{z^{1/m}\}$ is a **convergent** Puiseux series.

*Sketch of the theorem.* The Newton–Puiseux algorithm constructs roots iteratively via the Newton polygon $\mathcal{N}(Q)$, the lower convex hull of the points $(i,\operatorname{ord}_z a_i)$ in $\mathbb{R}^2$. Each segment of slope $-p/q$ (with $\gcd(p,q)=1$) determines candidate leading terms $c\,z^{p/q}$, where $c$ ranges over roots of the associated initial-form polynomial. One performs the substitution $T = c\,z^{p/q}+z^{p/q}\,T'$ — an **analytic** change of variable in $w=z^{1/q}$ — producing a new polynomial $Q_1(T')\in\mathbb{C}\{w\}[T']$ whose Newton polygon has strictly smaller height. Iterating, the denominators of exponents stabilize at some $m\leq d!$ after finitely many steps.

**Convergence** is the crucial point. At each step the substitution is analytic in $w=z^{1/q}$, so convergent coefficients remain convergent. When the Newton polygon eventually has a segment of slope $0$ with the initial form having a simple root, the **analytic implicit function theorem** directly yields a convergent power-series solution in $w$. Composing the finitely many analytic substitutions gives a convergent Puiseux series. $\square$

**Applying the theorem.** Let $f\in\mathbb{C}[[z]]$ satisfy $P(f)=0$. By the theorem, there exists $m\geq 1$ such that

$$P(T) = u_n(z)\prod_{j=1}^{n}\bigl(T-\varphi_j(z^{1/m})\bigr) \quad\text{in } \mathbb{C}\{z^{1/m}\}[T],$$

with each $\varphi_j\in\mathbb{C}\{z^{1/m}\}$ convergent. Via the natural inclusion $\mathbb{C}[[z]]\hookrightarrow\mathbb{C}[[z^{1/m}]]$, we view $f$ as an element of $\mathbb{C}[[z^{1/m}]]$ and evaluate:

$$0 = P(f) = u_n(z)\prod_{j=1}^{n}\bigl(f-\varphi_j(z^{1/m})\bigr) \quad\text{in } \mathbb{C}[[z^{1/m}]].$$

Since $u_n\neq 0$ and $\mathbb{C}[[z^{1/m}]]$ is an integral domain, there exists some $j$ with

$$f = \varphi_j(z^{1/m}) \quad\text{in } \mathbb{C}[[z^{1/m}]].$$

Now $\varphi_j\in\mathbb{C}\{z^{1/m}\}$ is a **convergent** Puiseux series, while $f\in\mathbb{C}[[z]]$ involves only **integer** powers of $z$. A convergent Puiseux series in $z^{1/m}$ that uses only integer powers of $z$ is, by definition, a convergent power series in $z$. Therefore

$$f \in \mathbb{C}\{z\},$$

i.e., $f$ is analytic. $\blacksquare$

